A||N||3||hoppe||88 GT brake calipers||||1||
Z||000000||hoppe||09-24-2000||06:45 PM||||My newly acquired 88 GT needs brake work. My wife took it to a local mechanic that said it needs rear calipers. They are not leaking and the rear brakes seem to work well when I tested them while baking up fast. The pads are thin though.<P>This mechanic did not remove the calipers so how would he know if they are bad. He said to me that the pistons will not go back into the housing because the e-brake is not often used.  I don't get it.<P>Also, the front clicks when applying the brakes and they are weak. The pads are definitely shot on the front but I could not see what would cause the clicking. You can feel it in the pedal<P>Now my wife is on me to do the work myself. ( It is really her car)<P>Any advice hear? Oh yeah- who has the best deals on rotors too?||||reg||1
Z||000001||maninblack||09-24-2000||07:23 PM||||check out your local GM store for rotors<BR>it should be pretty cheap,otherwise,if you want upgrade stuff, there should be plenty around. <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||09-24-2000||09:02 PM||||Your mechanic may not know how to get the pistons back in(you can screw them back in). Pick up a Haynes manual at your local Pep Boys or the like. Not the greatest manual but it tell how to change the brakes on the 88 (different than the 84-87) I doubt you need rear calipers and pulsation in the pedal is usually warped rotors.<BR>Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||09-25-2000||09:24 AM||||I agree with Phil, many shops are unfamilar with Fieros and are ignorant on them, especially brakes.<BR>The clicking you hear and feel may be the caused front the condition of the brakes as a whole. (clean and lube required) Go ahead and do the brake work and see if it goes away. If it doesen't go away, check you linkage to the master cylinder as it may need lube as well.||||reg||1
